File layout and gate ownership
For a hypothesis such as H03, the staged files are:

audit/hypotheses/H03/01_audit_and_plan.qmd;
audit/hypotheses/H03/02_implementation_and_v0_comparison.qmd;
audit/hypotheses/H03/production_computation.qmd, only when a production bootstrap or simulation requires a separate review gate;
notebooks/hypotheses/H03.qmd, the Stage 3 standalone reader-facing report; and
audit/hypotheses/H03/H03_analysis_preparation.qmd, the Stage 4 scientific description and provenance record for the result-producing pipeline.

Every source document is rendered to Quarto HTML for review. Exact model outputs, fitted-model samples, figure data, and table data remain stored as separate RDS or CSV artifacts and are linked from the relevant report.
The coordinating task owns shared preparation code, central ledgers, Quarto configuration, manuscript files, and the central gate-status table. A hypothesis task may prepare a gate report and a shared-change request, but it must stop rather than changing shared inputs or marking its own gate approved. After coordinator approval, the same hypothesis task may proceed to the next stage and edit only its stage-authorized Hxx scripts, tests, artifacts, audit files, and final hypothesis notebook.

Stage 1: Audit and proposed analysis
The first document audits the V0 analysis against:

the exact preregistered hypothesis and analysis specification;
all preregistration deviations stated before or in the submitted manuscript;
the implementation and results that actually produced V0; and
the analysis plan already developed during the Nature Health audit.

The report must explain, in plain language:

what question and quantity the analysis is intended to address;
what V0 actually estimated and how it handled repeated observations;
where V0 agrees with or differs from the preregistration and stated deviations;
any coding, data, model, multiplicity, diagnostic, figure, table, or claim problem found;
the proposed main analysis and why each retained or changed choice serves the analysis goal;
the exact primary and complementary roles of near-eye and chest-level measurements;
which registered sensitivities will later be needed; and
every decision that needs author approval before implementation.

The report contains visible evaluated R cells that print the exact Wilkinson formula objects used by V0 and proposed for the main analysis, including every full, reduced, null, or otherwise fitted comparison model. Distribution, link, contrasts, estimation method, correlation structure, and multiplicity families are stated beside those formulas because they are not encoded by Wilkinson notation.
Stage 1 does not select a method by looking for a preferred new result. It may reproduce V0 and run checks needed to establish whether its implementation is valid, but the proposed estimand and model are fixed before the new inferential results are examined.

Gate after Stage 1
Implementation starts only after the author approves the proposed estimand, sample, outcome construction, model formulas, response distribution, comparisons, multiplicity families, diagnostics, and placement roles. Any unresolved major choice keeps the hypothesis at Stage 1.

Stage 2: Implementation and comparison with V0
The approved analysis is implemented and run in a clean R session. If a computation-heavy bootstrap is required, this stage uses only the approved 50- or 100-replicate pilot and labels its intervals as preliminary and not for inference.
The second document must:

show the exact formulas supplied to every fitted model;
report the exact participants, participant-days, hours, observations, sites, and relevant category cells that entered each model;
report estimates in practical units with 95% confidence intervals and raw and adjusted p-values where applicable;
format every displayed raw or adjusted p-value under REPORT-008: a leading zero and three decimal places, &lt;0.001 for values strictly below 0.001, and bold type only when the value meets its explicitly labelled significance rule;
show convergence, singularity, residual, distributional, temporal, and influence checks relevant to the model;
display those diagnostics with a plain-language interpretation and state whether each fitted model is acceptable, acceptable with a stated limitation, or not acceptable for inference;
compare the main-analysis implementation, results, and defensible claims directly with V0;
apply the identical new implementation to the V0-prepared data and report that audit-only data sensitivity separately;
recreate the V0 figures and tables faithfully, then make only sensible, explicitly documented adjustments for correctness and readability;
produce the corresponding near-eye and chest-level outputs; and
export the exact data behind every durable figure and table.

Faithful recreation does not require preserving an error. When a V0 display cannot be retained, the report shows what changed and why. Site names, order, and colours follow config/site_display_registry.csv; metric names follow config/metric_display_registry.csv.
Expensive secondary work, including the full sensitivity battery, may remain out of Stage 2. The report must nevertheless list every deferred analysis and state whether it could alter the interpretation.

Gate after Stage 2
The author reviews the method, preliminary or final estimates, V0 comparison, recreated displays, and proposed reader-facing presentation. A material change reopens Stage 1. Otherwise the author approves either the production-computation gate or, when no production computation is needed, Stage 3.

Optional Stage 3: Production bootstrap or simulation
This stage is required whenever final inference depends on a computation-heavy bootstrap or simulation. It follows the separate pilot-first policy in audit/decisions/bootstrap_execution_policy.md.
After explicit approval of the pilot display and estimated runtime, the full run uses the same inputs, targets, formulas, resampling unit, failure rules, and output schema as the approved pilot. Its Quarto report records:

requested, successful, failed, and warning-producing replicates;
seeds, checkpoint behavior, elapsed time, and compute resources;
final intervals and other bootstrap-derived quantities;
any difference between the pilot preview and the production presentation;
updated main-versus-V0 and V0-prepared-data comparisons; and
all diagnostics required before the result can be treated as final, together with their interpretation and an explicit acceptable, acceptable-with-limitations, or not-acceptable assessment.

Pilot estimates are never substituted for production estimates. A code, model, target, or display change after the pilot requires a new pilot and a new runtime approval.

Gate after the production run
The final reader-facing report is blocked until the production outputs pass their checks and the author approves them. Material instability or a changed scientific conclusion reopens the relevant earlier stage.

Final stage: Reader-facing hypothesis report
The final notebooks/hypotheses/Hxx.qmd stands on its own. It contains no mention of V0, submitted-versus-new comparisons, discarded candidates, working variants, or the construction history of the analysis.
In concise, accessible language, it presents:

the question, including the exact preregistered hypothesis as a quotation;
what was analysed and why the chosen method addresses the question;
every deviation from the preregistration, listed explicitly;
the result in prose, figures, and digestible tables;
the exact model sample and uncertainty needed to interpret the result;
a restrained interpretation and the important limitations; and
how the conclusion changes under the registered scientific sensitivities.

The report also shows the model diagnostics needed to judge the analysis. It explains what the diagnostic displays or summaries indicate and states plainly whether the near-eye and chest-level models are acceptable, acceptable with specified limitations, or not acceptable. Diagnostic output without interpretation is not sufficient.
Near-eye measurements remain primary. Every hypothesis also has a clearly labelled chest-level section that recreates the primary figures and tables with chest-level data, rather than merely referring to an audit table or listing chest coefficients in prose.
Reader-facing tables use gt. They are divided into conceptually coherent, legible pieces rather than continued across several pages or filled with audit-only columns. Complete numerical results remain available as linked CSV files. Figures and tables use familiar manuscript terminology, melEDI in reader-facing text, the registered site display rules, and 95% confidence intervals wherever they are estimable.
Necessary technical terms are explained on first use. Code may be folded, but each analytical section states what was done, why it was done, what the display shows, and what the result means.

Final preparation and provenance companion
After the reader-facing result and its accepted artifacts are fixed, the same hypothesis task creates audit/hypotheses/Hxx/Hxx_analysis_preparation.qmd. This companion is not an additional scientific stage or approval gate. It explains how to audit and reproduce the accepted result without reintroducing V0 comparisons into the result report.
The companion follows the H02 convention:

a Mermaid diagram traces approved inputs through hypothesis-specific model data, fitted models, diagnostics, sensitivities, displays, and claims;
an explicit execution-boundary callout states what rendering verifies and that it does not refit models, predict, bootstrap, simulate, or recompute other expensive results;
concise tables and plots summarize inputs, fitted samples, temporal or metric support, central parameters, model formulas, diagnostics, and sensitivity branches;
separate maps identify executable scripts, produced artifacts, and stage manifests in analytical order;
the companion and notebooks/hypotheses/Hxx.qmd link to each other; and
a hypothesis-specific preparation manifest records the companion’s inputs and outputs while excluding the final worker inventory to avoid circular checksums.

The coordinating task adds the companion to the Nature Health render list and navigation immediately after its result page only after the source exists. The hypothesis task then refreshes its preparation manifest, non-circular worker inventory, structural tests, and handoff against the website-rendered identities. This documentation-only closure must not rerun a scientific model or reopen an accepted result unless it exposes a substantive discrepancy.

Optional production-computation gate between Stages 2 and 3
This stage is required whenever final inference depends on a computation-heavy bootstrap or simulation. It follows the separate pilot-first policy in audit/decisions/bootstrap_execution_policy.md.
After explicit approval of the pilot display and estimated runtime, the full run uses the same inputs, targets, formulas, resampling unit, failure rules, and output schema as the approved pilot. Its Quarto report records:

requested, successful, failed, and warning-producing replicates;
seeds, checkpoint behavior, elapsed time, and compute resources;
final intervals and other bootstrap-derived quantities;
any difference between the pilot preview and the production presentation;
updated main-versus-V0 and V0-prepared-data comparisons; and
all diagnostics required before the result can be treated as final, together with their interpretation and an explicit acceptable, acceptable-with-limitations, or not-acceptable assessment.

Pilot estimates are never substituted for production estimates. A code, model, target, or display change after the pilot requires a new pilot and a new runtime approval.

Gate after the production run
The final reader-facing report is blocked until the production outputs pass their checks and the author approves them. Material instability or a changed scientific conclusion reopens the relevant earlier stage.

Required comparison content
H01 and H02 retain the comparison order already in use. For H03–H11, the following content is distributed across the gated documents rather than forced into one page:

the question and exact comparison being estimated;
what the manuscript reported;
the main analysis;
a direct submitted-versus-main table covering magnitude, direction, uncertainty, statistical conclusion, analytical sample, and claim;
the gap-timing-unaware dataset sensitivity using the same new implementation for both datasets;
the reason for every method or data-preparation change;
diagnostics and other registered sensitivities;
relation to the preregistration; and
verification status, decisions still needed, and conditions that require a new review.

A preregistered method is never substituted for the method represented in the manuscript merely because the two differ.

Method comparison
The method comparison explicitly covers:

which data release and file were used, how rows were matched, and any correction to source records;
sensor placement and analysis scenario;
inclusion rules and the denominator used to calculate a proportion;
outcome, predictor, contrast, transformation, and unit;
whether the analysis concerns participants, participant-days, hours, or individual observations, and how repeated observations are handled;
the assumed outcome distribution and scale, the fixed and random model terms, and how correlation between observations—including observations close together in time—is handled;
the set of related tests adjusted together for multiple testing and the adjustment method;
checks of model fit, influential observations, and whether model fitting completed stably; and
code, fitted-model file, result table, figure, and the exact data used to produce the figure.

The gap-timing-unaware dataset sensitivity must use the same new analysis implementation as the main analysis. Only its prepared-data input differs. Each correction made to the gap-timing-unaware dataset has an identifier and evidence locator. A model cannot be simplified only for this sensitivity.
For every multiple-testing correction, the hypothesis page must state which single test is selected from each outcome model before the results are examined. Individual coefficients and follow-up contrasts are not automatically added to the same set of tests.

Exact formula display
Every H01–H11 audit page must contain a visible, evaluated R code cell that prints the exact Wilkinson formula objects supplied to the fitting functions. The display must include the selected model and every fitted model used in a likelihood, Wald, score, AIC, or other model-structure comparison, including full, reduced, null, and sensitivity models.
The displayed formulas must be generated from the same formula-building function or recovered directly with stats::formula() from the fitted model; a separately typed mathematical equation is explanatory but does not satisfy this requirement. A generic response ~ ... formula may represent several outcomes only when their right-hand side, random effects, smooths, offsets, and correlation structure are identical. If any of these differ, each distinct formula must be shown and linked to its model ID.
Wilkinson notation does not encode the response distribution, link, estimation method, contrasts, or residual-correlation settings. Those must be shown immediately beside the formula output and remain in the model manifest. The formula cell and manifest must agree exactly before a hypothesis audit can be marked complete.


Pilot before a computation-heavy bootstrap
Every computation-heavy bootstrap or simulation must first run a separate 50- or 100-replicate pilot with the proposed production code. The pilot is used to verify failure handling, checkpointing, output tables and figures, and to estimate the duration and resource needs of the full run. It is labelled as non-inferential and cannot supply manuscript values or claims.
Before the full run starts, the hypothesis task must show the pilot output, report successful and failed replicates, provide a reasoned full-runtime range and expected completion time, and obtain explicit author confirmation. Any code, diagnostic or display repair requires another pilot. The complete rule and the exception for the H01 run already underway are recorded in bootstrap_execution_policy.md.
Each difference is assigned a change_class. none is an affirmative result and means that the submitted method was reproduced and retained. A change is not called a repair unless the evidence and verification justify that classification.

Result comparison
The direct submitted-versus-main table records, where applicable:

the exact fitted-model counts—not counts from the prepared dataset—for participants, participant-days, hours, 30-minute observations, other observations, and sites;
valid measurement hours used to derive a participant-day or participant-level outcome, clearly separated from observations that entered the fitted model;
category counts, including category × site counts when an interaction or sparse-cell decision is involved;
exact common-sample counts, where a common sample means that both analyses use the same participants, participant-days, or time-resolved observations;
estimate, standard error, confidence interval, raw and adjusted (p)-values, the set of related tests adjusted together, and the result’s position within that set;
changes in direction, uncertainty, statistical significance, or conclusion;
the submitted manuscript claim and the main-analysis claim; and
links to the exact submitted and main-analysis code and output files.

The accompanying data-sensitivity table repeats the result using the same new H01–H11 implementation on the gap-timing-unaware dataset. It reports both model samples, their exact common sample, estimates and 95% confidence intervals, raw and adjusted (p)-values, diagnostics, and a stability classification.

P-value display
Every displayed raw or adjusted p-value follows REPORT-008: use a leading zero and exactly three decimal places, show values strictly below 0.001 as &lt;0.001, and use bold type only when that displayed value meets its explicitly labelled significance rule. Raw and adjusted values are labelled separately. Full numeric precision remains in CSV and model artifacts; display rounding is never used to decide significance. The shared helper is scripts/pipeline/p_value_display.R.
The numerical values take precedence over a qualitative label. A difference is described as negligible or material only when a practical margin was declared before the comparison. Otherwise it is not_classified_no_margin.


Paired near-eye and chest comparison
Each Stage 3 report applies REPORT-009. When separately fitted near-eye and chest estimates exist for the identical paired/common sample, outcome, predictor, contrast, model structure, transformation, and scale, show their relationship with near-eye on the horizontal axis, chest on the vertical axis, an identity line, null lines, and equal axis geometry. Component intervals may appear in an adjacent table when including them in the plot would be illegible.
Do not compare unmatched all-available samples in this display or treat closeness to the identity line as equivalence. When a scalar comparison would be invalid, state why and use a matched curve, contrast plot, coefficient plot, or concise table instead. Retain paired source data, an informative caption, and alt text.

When author approval is needed
Author approval is required before adopting a change to the comparison being estimated, inclusion rule, metric, statistical model, set of tests adjusted together, substantive result, preregistration interpretation, or manuscript claim. A hypothesis review cannot be completed until:

its submitted method and result are recoverable or explicitly marked otherwise;
its primary and gap-timing-unaware sensitivity code and output pass a run in a fresh R session;
every correction to the gap-timing-unaware dataset passes the data-fidelity rule or is removed;
method and result rows are complete and linked;
relevant diagnostics and sensitivities are classified;
consequential manuscript claims are linked to verified results; and
mandatory checks are PASS.

A change in an earlier preparation or analysis step requires every affected hypothesis to be reviewed again.

9. How to examine or reproduce H05
A reader can follow the analysis in five passes:

inspect H05_input_audit.csv, h05_contract.R, and the factor, metric, formula, and run registries to verify the frozen inputs and specifications;
compare the exact samples above with H05_model_frame_index.csv and the 136 frames in H05_model_frames.rds;
follow run_h05_stage2.R through h05_modeling.R, then the bounded MDER and L10 reseal scripts, to connect each frame to its current model object, coefficient, interval, likelihood-ratio test, diagnostic, and sensitivity record;
inspect the complete 68-value family audit before interpreting any individual association; and
read the H05 results report alongside its tables, figures, and paired source data.

The most important boundary is that the preparation record documents a frozen analysis; it does not create an alternate result. If an input, metric rule, response model, or estimand changes, the affected scientific pipeline must be rerun before this page is refreshed.

9. How to examine or reproduce H08
A reader can follow the analysis in six passes:

inspect the METRIC-011 decision, shared evidence manifest, H08 result comparison, and protected-scope reconciliation for the exact-zero maintenance boundary;
inspect H08_input_audit.csv, h08_contract.R, and the score, metric, formula, and run registries to verify frozen inputs and specifications;
compare the exact samples above with H08_model_frame_index.csv and the 108 frames in H08_model_frames.rds;
follow run_h08_stage2.R through h08_modeling.R to connect each frame to its stored object, practical effect, 95% interval, likelihood-ratio test, diagnostic, and sensitivity record;
inspect all eight complete nine-value multiplicity families before interpreting an individual association; and
read the H08 results report alongside its numerical tables, accessible figures, and exact source CSV files.

This preparation record documents a frozen analysis. If an input, score rule, metric definition, sample rule, response model, or estimand changes, the affected scientific pipeline must be rerun before this page is refreshed.

Preregistration context and source data
The preregistration-deviations page records why the selected main H06 report uses the hourly estimand and this daily-metric report is complementary. The same page records the primary predictor scope, the model and site structure, and the multiplicity and contrast decisions.
Reader source data:


complete primary results;

complete complementary placement results;

complete gap-timing-unaware results;

primary site-specific interaction estimates;

mutually adjusted common-sample stability results;

mutually adjusted site estimates;

main hourly H06 comparison;

30-minute temporal GAMM summary;

12-family primary and sensitivity summary; and

model-check classification summary.

Preregistration deviations


DEV-035: each metric’s study-site-average VLSQ-8 association is estimated separately from its VLSQ-8-by-site interaction;

DEV-036: average associations and site interactions use separate complete nine-metric FDR families, with no retained result in either family.
